  "record": {
    "read_status": "partial",
    "file_problem": "Repo metadata, README, and file list only; code and other files were not read or run.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The README describes using directional ablation, explicitly repurposed from refusal abliteration, to reduce PII echo. Its reported results concern PII recall, not refusal behavior or the refusal direction.",
    "gate_note": "The README reports PII-retention, perplexity, and coherence results for ablation of PII directions. These are not results about the refusal direction or refusal behavior, so they do not test V1–V8. The README's comparison to the refusal-abliteration technique is not itself a result about refusal.",
    "test_reason": "The project reuses abliteration, a difference-in-means direction projected out of the weights, against a PII-recall direction in Qwen1.5-0.5B-Chat. It reports only PII retention, perplexity and coherence. No refusal direction, refusal rate or harmful/harmless contrast is measured, so no version V1–V8 is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The README says M2 preserves coherence best but reduces PII echo least, while M1 and M3 fully suppress PII at a perplexity cost, framed as a privacy–utility tradeoff. However, the README's own numbers show M2 increasing PII retention above baseline (50–75% vs 25%), not reducing it slightly.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The README describes a PII-focused project, not a replication of refusal-direction work: it finds PII directions and projects them from selected weight matrices, using three methods and a strength sweep. It calls the approach a repurposing of abliteration.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported PII retention, perplexity, and coherence vary by method and ablation strength.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Qwen1.5-0.5B-Chat-4bit, M1 and M3 PII-direction weight projection drop PII retention from 25.0% to 0.0%, with perplexity rising to 142–15496. M2 multi-layer projection raises retention to 50–75% while keeping perplexity at 14.2–34.2.",
        "proposed_claim": "Abliteration-style projection of a PII direction can suppress PII echo, at large method- and strength-dependent perplexity cost; the multi-layer Gaussian variant does not reduce PII retention.",
